WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:06.090
Kong: Skull Island is packed with eye candy
and giant monster fights, but director Jordan

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:10.140
Vogt-Roberts also stuffed the movie full of
pop culture Easter eggs, referencing everything

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.600
from anime to Jurassic Park.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:13.480
"Hold onto your butts."

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.900
"Hooold onto your butts."

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.840
And Kong is especially full of video game
Easter eggs.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.780
Here are all the ones you missed in Kong:
Skull Island.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:24.380
Metal Gear Solid

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.680
Jordan Vogt-Roberts is a massive Metal Gear
Solid fan.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:28.820
He told Den of Geek:

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:31.880
"That's one of the most important franchises
to me on the planet.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.880
Not even just as a video game, but as a piece
of media."

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.100
So it's no surprise that the director couldn't
help but put a little bit of Metal Gear into

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:39.700
Skull Island.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.300
Remember the name of the ramshackle boat that
John C. Reilly's character builds?

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.920
"She's called the Gray Fox."

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.980
If that sounds familiar to you, you've probably
played a lot of video games.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.720
Gray Fox is a recurring character in the Metal
Gear franchise, and according to Vogt-Roberts,

00:00:54.729 --> 00:00:58.379
Gray Fox was only supposed to be the tip of
the iceberg when it came to Skull Island's

00:00:58.380 --> 00:00:59.820
Metal Gear Solid references.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.200
Screenwriter Dan Gilroy wrote a sequence in
which Tom Hiddleston's character breaks into

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:08.700
a military base, and Vogt-Roberts claims he
was going to "get very Metal Gear with it"

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:09.960
before the scene was cut.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:11.720
The Legend of Zelda

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:16.350
Samuel L. Jackson's group of pilots, the Sky
Devils, get a lot of screen-time throughout

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:16.860
Kong.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:21.060
That gives viewers plenty of time to study
their uniforms—and Zelda fans may have noticed

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.360
that the Sky Devils insignia looks a lot like
the Hyrule Wingcrest, which made its first

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.860
appearance in The Legend of Zelda: The Ocarina
of Time (my favorite game of all time, FYI).

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:36.500
The resemblance isn't exact, but according
to Vogt-Roberts, the similarity is 100% intentional.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:37.720
Pokémon

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.320
Skull Island's scariest creatures aren't giant
apes or bamboo spiders—they're those freaky

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:43.760
two-legged reptiles.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.160
"The Iwis won't speak their name.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.260
But I call them Skullcrawlers."

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:49.160
"Why?"

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:51.060
"Cuz it sounds neat."

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.580
But despite their ferociousness, the Skullcrawlers
have a fairly cuddly origin.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.340
According to a behind-the-scenes feature at
Wired, Vogt-Roberts gave the special effects

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.720
team a single reference to get them started:
Cubone, one of the very first Pokémon.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:07.960
It's the kind of thing that's impossible to
unsee: both creatures have long heads that

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.200
look like skulls and lizard-like bodies.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:14.639
Of course, there's one big difference between
the two: the Skullcrawlers just look like

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.240
they're wearing another creature's skull.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:21.000
Cubone actually is—the skull belongs to
his mother, which is much more terrifying.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.080
Mega Man and Metroid

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:27.360
The Legend of Zelda isn't the only big video
game franchise that snuck into the costumes

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:28.480
of Kong: Skull Island.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.389
Some of the helmets donned by the pilots in
the movie resemble those worn by the heroes

00:02:32.389 --> 00:02:35.400
in Mega Man, Metroid, and the classic Gundam
franchise.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.760
Of course, Samus and Mega Man sport helmets
with blocky extra parts.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.860
The flight helmets in Skull Island are a lot
more realistic, and you have to study the

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.060
designs closely to see the similarities.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:49.940
3D extrusions like the caps around Mega Man's
ears have been reduced to 2D paintjobs, and

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:51.760
the color schemes are totally different.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:56.660
Still, if you look hard enough, you'll notice
that many of Mega Man and Samus' iconic silhouettes

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:57.940
reappear on the helmets.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:01.520
That's enough for us to count it, even if
the geeks in us wish that the similarities

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:03.280
were just a little more pronounced.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:04.560
Resident Evil

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:09.480
Kong: Skull Island introduced King Kong with
a stunning 360-degree shot that zooms through

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.600
several helicopter cockpits while circling
the giant ape.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:16.660
It's an exciting way to highlight Skull Island's
biggest star, and in the context of the movie,

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:20.500
it goes a long way towards showing just how
big Kong really is.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:21.340
Well, guess what?

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.299
Vogt-Roberts totally stole the shot from a
video game.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:25.500
He's not shy about it, either.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:26.020
He said,

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:27.920
"That's a very video game shot to me.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.540
That shot actually was inspired by a cinematic
in a Resident Evil game."

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:36.159
Vogt-Roberts doesn't identify which Resident
Evil he's ripping off—there are a lot of

00:03:36.159 --> 00:03:40.559
games out there, and almost as many helicopter
crashes in them—but fans think he's probably

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.500
talking about Operation Raccoon City, a non-canon
third-person shooter.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:45.980
Shadow of the Colossus

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:50.200
If you're making a movie about giant monsters,
it makes sense that you'd look to the most

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:53.180
famous video game about giant monsters for
inspiration.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.140
That's exactly what Vogt-Roberts did.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:56.319
He told Den of Geek:

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:00.460
"Shadow Of The Colossus was a huge influence
on me on this movie….the colossi in those

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:03.660
games have such a slow, melancholy, morose
quality to them.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:08.260
And the sense of scale...I think and I hope
that Kong has a similar vibe in the film."

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:12.340
In other words, if you go into Kong: Skull
Island looking for visuals borrowed from Shadow

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:13.980
of the Colossus, you won't find them.

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:18.730
If you think of Kong as spiritual descendent
of the titular Colossi, however, you'll spot

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:21.000
Shadow of the Colossus' influence throughout
the film.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:24.420
Like Shadow's Colossi, Kong is a majestic
and tragic figure.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.820
It's no surprise that they go hand in hand.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:27.860
Journey

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:31.980
In Journey, your player has to cross a vast
desert, trying to reach a glowing mountain

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:32.960
in the distance.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:36.960
You can connect with other players, but you
can only communicate with actions or musical

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:37.480
notes.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:41.560
It's a strange, ethereal game that's been
called "a kind of non-denominational religious

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:43.040
experience for players."

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:47.500
Kong director Jordan Vogt-Roberts' review
isn't quite so wordy, but gives the game just

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:48.140
as much praise:

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.880
"Journey is one of my favorite things… ever."

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.540
As such, Vogt-Roberts snuck Journey's illuminated
mountain into Skull Island's landscape.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:59.620
The peaks are a little more jagged in Kong,
but the silhouette is unmistakable.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:00.860
Thanks for watching!

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.720
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.000
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

